    {
      "to": "miguel.alvarez@stateu.edu",
      "subject": "Guest lecture on agentic programming?",
      "message": "Dear Professor Alvarez,\n\nThank you for the kind invitation \u2014 we're delighted that your students are exploring Jaseci, and we would be glad to give a guest lecture on agentic programming for your AI systems seminar.\n\nSeptember 22 works well on our end. A 60-minute presentation within the 2\u20134pm Eastern slot is perfect; we're happy to leave time for Q&A either within or after the 60 minutes, whichever you prefer. Remote delivery via Zoom or your preferred platform is no problem at all.\n\nI'll confirm the specific speaker from our team shortly and coordinate with you on any particular topics or areas of focus you'd like us to cover. If your students have specific questions or projects they're working on in Jaseci, feel free to share those ahead of time so we can tailor the lecture accordingly.\n\nWe'll be in touch soon with the speaker details and a proposed outline.\n\nBest regards,\nThe Jaseci Team\nimpact@jaseci.org"
    },
    {
      "to": "priya.nair@devfleet.io",
      "subject": "Re: Quick question | byLLM structured output",
      "message": "Hi Priya,\n\nThanks for reaching out \u2014 great use case for byLLM.\n\nYes, byLLM supports typed/structured outputs. You can define a JSON schema and the model will return fields conforming to that schema directly, so your team won't need to parse plain text.\n\nFor your log triage pipeline, you'd define a schema like:\n\n{\n  \"severity\": \"string\",   // e.g., \"ERROR\", \"WARN\", \"INFO\"\n  \"component\": \"string\",  // e.g., \"walker_spawn\", \"node_create\"\n  \"message\": \"string\"     // e.g., \"context missing\", \"slow response\"\n}\n\nYou pass this schema as part of your request, and byLLM returns a structured object per log line. For batch processing, you can also request an array of these objects to triage multiple lines in a single call.\n\nA few notes for your setup:\n\n- The severity field can be constrained to an enum (ERROR, WARN, INFO, etc.) to ensure consistent output.\n- For high-throughput pipelines, batching multiple log lines per request reduces latency and cost.\n- If a log line doesn't fit the expected pattern, the model will still populate the fields to the best of its ability \u2014 you may want to add a confidence or \"unparsed\" flag if that's a concern for your pipeline.\n\nHappy to set up a quick call or share a sample config if that would help your evaluation.\n\nBest,\nJaseci Team"
    }
  ],
